WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:02.640 --> 00:00:09.120
In July 1216, Prince Louis of France set up his&nbsp;
siege artillery before the walls of Dover Castle.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:09.120 --> 00:00:13.280
The French[RS2.1] prince had come to England to&nbsp;
support a group of rebel barons against King John&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:13.280 --> 00:00:18.800
of England, occupying much of the country and&nbsp;
now aiming to capture Dover Castle, known as&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:18.800 --> 00:00:24.640
“the key to England.” The castle controlled the&nbsp;
shortest sea route to France and thus Louis’s&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:24.640 --> 00:00:30.560
connection to his logistical base at home. The&nbsp;
siege of Dover Castle was characterized by the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:30.560 --> 00:00:37.200
use of new types of artillery, brutal assaults,&nbsp;
and near-impenetrable defenses. The very future&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:37.200 --> 00:00:43.520
of England hung in the balance: what began as&nbsp;
a dispute over the barons’ rights, enshrined in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:43.520 --> 00:00:50.560
the famous Magna Carta, quickly escalated into a&nbsp;
full-scale French invasion—and Dover Castle stood&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:50.560 --> 00:00:56.880
as one of the last strongholds of resistance. This&nbsp;
is how modern historiography tells the story of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:56.880 --> 00:01:07.520
the staggering siege of Dover Castle.
Chapter 1: Magna Carta&nbsp;

00:01:07.520 --> 00:01:13.680
This is Prince John—the villain of many Robin&nbsp;
Hood tales. His representation as a greedy, cruel&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:13.680 --> 00:01:19.600
oppressor has real historical roots, for many&nbsp;
of his contemporaries considered the real Prince&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:19.600 --> 00:01:27.520
John—King John “Lackland”—a tyrant. In 1204, John&nbsp;
lost much of the Angevin Empire to Philip II of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:27.520 --> 00:01:34.000
France and spent the rest of his reign trying to&nbsp;
win it back. To fund these costly campaigns, he&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:34.000 --> 00:01:39.520
imposed heavy extra taxes on top of the legitimate&nbsp;
feudal levies, collecting them through methods&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:39.520 --> 00:01:46.160
that were, to put it mildly, questionable. These&nbsp;
relentless demands pushed some of his barons—royal&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:46.160 --> 00:01:52.960
vassals with vast estates—toward financial ruin,&nbsp;
fueling growing resentment. The breaking point&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:52.960 --> 00:01:59.040
came when John arbitrarily turned against one of&nbsp;
his closest allies, William de Braose, forcing&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:59.040 --> 00:02:05.840
him into exile. By 1212, several barons were&nbsp;
conspiring “to drive the king and his family from&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:05.840 --> 00:02:12.240
the kingdom and choose someone else as king in his&nbsp;
place,” even plotting to assassinate him during a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:12.240 --> 00:02:20.240
campaign in Wales. When the conspiracy was exposed&nbsp;
and the attempt foiled, their anger only deepened.&nbsp;

00:02:23.040 --> 00:02:28.720
When the king returned from yet another failed&nbsp;
campaign in France in 1214 and immediately sought&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:28.720 --> 00:02:35.760
to collect the due scutage to fund the next war,&nbsp;
tempers boiled over. The barons refused to pay,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:35.760 --> 00:02:42.560
arguing that financing ongoing campaigns in&nbsp;
France went beyond their feudal obligations.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:42.560 --> 00:02:47.760
That November, around 40 disaffected nobles&nbsp;
gathered in the abbey church of Bury St.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:47.760 --> 00:02:53.280
Edmunds and swore not to support the king until&nbsp;
he confirmed the traditional liberties that his&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:53.280 --> 00:02:59.600
great-grandfather, Henry I, had granted at&nbsp;
his coronation. Essentially, they sought to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:59.600 --> 00:03:05.600
define their rights as vassals and set clear&nbsp;
limits on the king’s authority over them.&nbsp;

00:03:05.600 --> 00:03:11.920
In early 1215, the rebellious barons brought&nbsp;
their demands to the king in London. John deeply&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:11.920 --> 00:03:17.200
resented the proposed limits on his authority—and&nbsp;
the fact that the rebels appeared before him in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:17.200 --> 00:03:24.080
arms, an act that was essentially a declaration of&nbsp;
war. Nevertheless, he promised to meet with them&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:24.080 --> 00:03:31.120
after Easter to resolve the dispute. In truth,&nbsp;
neither side believed a settlement was likely,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:31.120 --> 00:03:39.040
and both began preparing for civil war.
When John declared his intention to join a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:39.040 --> 00:03:44.880
crusade to Palestine on March 4 and canceled&nbsp;
his meeting with the barons, open revolt&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:44.880 --> 00:03:51.600
erupted. The rebels renounced their oath of&nbsp;
fealty and took up arms. Led by Robert FitzWalter,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:51.600 --> 00:03:57.920
one of John’s fiercest enemies, they laid siege to&nbsp;
the royal castle at Northampton. John immediately&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:57.920 --> 00:04:04.400
called in his mercenary forces from Ireland and&nbsp;
Flanders, but their march was slow. In mid-May,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:04.400 --> 00:04:10.080
before he could gather enough troops, the City of&nbsp;
London—home to the treasury and exchequer—threw&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:10.080 --> 00:04:16.240
its support behind the rebels. From that moment,&nbsp;
their cause gained momentum overnight, and the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:16.240 --> 00:04:23.440
king realized he had no choice but to negotiate.&nbsp;
On May 27, the two sides agreed to a truce.&nbsp;

00:04:23.440 --> 00:04:29.600
Two weeks later, on June 10, 1215[EE3.1][RS3.2],&nbsp;
John met the barons at Runnymede on the Thames.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:29.600 --> 00:04:35.040
Over five days of negotiations, they drafted a&nbsp;
document—based on The Articles of the Barons,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:35.040 --> 00:04:38.240
sometimes called The Forty-Eight&nbsp;
Articles[EE4.1][RS4.2]—setting out&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:38.240 --> 00:04:44.560
their liberties, rights, and privileges. This&nbsp;
“Great Charter,” or Magna Carta Libertatum,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:44.560 --> 00:04:51.680
would come to be seen as the cornerstone of&nbsp;
English constitutional law. On June 15, 1215,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:51.680 --> 00:04:57.760
King John affixed his seal to the Magna Carta,&nbsp;
agreeing to most of the barons’ demands.&nbsp;

00:04:57.760 --> 00:05:03.520
The settlement temporarily ended the dispute,&nbsp;
and most of the nobles renewed their oaths of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:03.520 --> 00:05:09.600
allegiance to the king. But the peace was&nbsp;
fragile: neither side trusted the other,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:09.600 --> 00:05:16.160
and John had no real intention of enforcing the&nbsp;
reforms or curbing his authority in the long term.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:16.160 --> 00:05:22.480
He quickly appealed to Pope Innocent III, who on&nbsp;
August 24 declared the Magna Carta void on the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:22.480 --> 00:05:29.120
grounds that it had been imposed under duress.&nbsp;
The Pope also excommunicated the rebels. With&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:29.120 --> 00:05:34.560
this legal and spiritual backing, John advanced&nbsp;
on London at the head of his mercenary army,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:34.560 --> 00:05:46.771
determined to crush the uprising once&nbsp;
and for all. The civil war had begun.&nbsp;

00:05:46.771 --> 00:05:49.680
Chapter 2: The Key to England
John advanced as far as the River Medway, where&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:49.680 --> 00:05:55.600
Rochester Castle—occupied by the rebels—blocked&nbsp;
his path. After his troops were repelled at the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:55.600 --> 00:05:58.978
old bridge crossing, the king laid siege to the&nbsp;
castle. The fierce defense by the small garrison&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:58.978 --> 00:06:00.640
at Rochester was later popularized in the 2011&nbsp;
film Ironclad. When Rochester Castle finally fell&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:00.640 --> 00:06:07.760
on November 30, 1215, John had gathered a strong&nbsp;
army. However, instead of pressing on toward&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:07.760 --> 00:06:13.280
London, he turned north. He marched through&nbsp;
central England to Berwick-upon-Tweed,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:13.280 --> 00:06:20.240
which he captured on January 15, 1216, then moved&nbsp;
south again through Colchester in East Anglia.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:20.240 --> 00:06:25.040
Despite this successful campaign,&nbsp;
the resistance only grew stronger.&nbsp;

00:06:25.040 --> 00:06:30.960
During John’s campaign, the barons—struggling&nbsp;
militarily—reached out to his archenemy,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:30.960 --> 00:06:38.400
Philip II of France. In exchange for support,&nbsp;
they offered the English throne to Philip’s son,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:38.400 --> 00:06:44.720
Louis, whose wife, Blanche of Castile, was&nbsp;
a descendant of King Henry II of England.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:44.720 --> 00:06:50.800
Louis agreed and sent a contingent of knights to&nbsp;
London as a vanguard. Despite his father’s advice&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:50.800 --> 00:06:57.040
against the move and threats of excommunication&nbsp;
from the Pope, Louis began planning an invasion.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:57.040 --> 00:07:06.880
What started as a war for the barons’ liberties&nbsp;
soon turned into a battle for the English crown.&nbsp;

00:07:06.880 --> 00:07:12.960
The French invasion began in the spring of 1216.&nbsp;
One foggy day, the watchmen along the Kent coast&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:12.960 --> 00:07:18.720
spotted dozens of sails on the English&nbsp;
Channel; soon after, an army came ashore.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:18.720 --> 00:07:24.240
When the king heard the news, he withdrew westward&nbsp;
to Winchester. To block further French landings,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:24.240 --> 00:07:29.440
he stationed part of the English fleet—expanded&nbsp;
and modernized during his reign—off the Thames&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:29.440 --> 00:07:36.800
estuary. But on May 18, a violent storm severely&nbsp;
damaged his ships, allowing Prince Louis to land&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:36.800 --> 00:07:43.440
additional troops at Pegwell Bay near Sandwich.
Louis immediately marched toward London.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:43.440 --> 00:07:49.440
Canterbury surrendered without a fight, and&nbsp;
Rochester Castle fell after a brief siege,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:49.440 --> 00:07:55.280
clearing the way for the French prince to enter&nbsp;
the capital on June 2. The rebels welcomed him&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:55.280 --> 00:08:00.080
as a liberator and proclaimed him king&nbsp;
at St. Paul’s Cathedral, though he was&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:00.080 --> 00:08:07.440
not crowned yet. Louis then pursued King John,&nbsp;
reaching Winchester on July 14 and capturing&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:07.440 --> 00:08:12.960
it after several days of bombardment with his&nbsp;
siege engines. With this victory, the rebels&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:12.960 --> 00:08:18.480
and their French allies controlled the two most&nbsp;
important cities in southern England. Now, Louis&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:18.480 --> 00:08:24.800
set his sights on the fortress that controlled&nbsp;
the crucial link to his homeland: Dover Castle.&nbsp;

00:08:24.800 --> 00:08:30.560
The English historian Matthew of Paris once&nbsp;
described Dover Castle as Clavis Angliae—the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:30.560 --> 00:08:36.000
“Key to England.” Commanding the Strait of&nbsp;
Dover, the fortress was both a strategic&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:36.000 --> 00:08:41.440
stronghold and a vital communications hub.&nbsp;
As long as it remained in English hands,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:41.440 --> 00:08:47.120
it threatened Louis’s lifeline to his logistical&nbsp;
base in France. If he wanted to ensure his&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:47.120 --> 00:08:57.280
reinforcements would not be met with closed gates,&nbsp;
he had to seize the key to England—at any cost.&nbsp;

00:08:57.280 --> 00:09:05.040
In July 1216, French troops marched up to Dover&nbsp;
Castle. What they saw was awe-inspiring. Perched&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:05.040 --> 00:09:11.440
like a stone giant on a coastal hill above the&nbsp;
town, the castle was unrivaled in England. At&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:11.440 --> 00:09:18.080
its heart stood the rectangular keep—about 30&nbsp;
by 30 meters—a formidable stronghold in its&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:18.080 --> 00:09:25.120
own right. Surrounding the inner courtyard was&nbsp;
a massive stone curtain wall dotted with towers,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:25.120 --> 00:09:32.080
while further out, a huge U-shaped circular wall&nbsp;
stretched toward the coast. This outer defense&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:32.080 --> 00:09:37.120
was broken only by a gatehouse flanked&nbsp;
by two towers on the northwest side.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:37.680 --> 00:09:43.200
Next to the gate stood several flanking&nbsp;
towers, and below, separated by a deep moat,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:43.200 --> 00:09:48.480
lay the barbican—a fortified gateway&nbsp;
enclosed by a wooden rampart and its own&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:48.480 --> 00:09:54.560
moat. To enter the castle from the barbican,&nbsp;
attackers had to cross a steep, narrow wooden&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:54.560 --> 00:10:01.920
bridge. This gateway was nearly impregnable.
The formidable fortress was commanded by the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:01.920 --> 00:10:08.640
seasoned knight Hubert de Burgh, the justiciar of&nbsp;
England and perhaps John’s most loyal supporter.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:08.640 --> 00:10:14.880
De Burgh had earned his reputation in 1205&nbsp;
by holding Chinon Castle for nearly a year&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:14.880 --> 00:10:21.280
against the vastly superior forces of the French&nbsp;
king. At Dover, he had around 140 knights and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:21.280 --> 00:10:26.960
men-at-arms under his command and had stocked&nbsp;
the castle with ample supplies and ammunition.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:26.960 --> 00:10:42.126
Taking Dover Castle would be a Herculean task—but&nbsp;
Louis was confident he could accomplish it.&nbsp;

00:10:42.126 --> 00:10:43.760
Chapter 3: The Siege of Dover
