00:11:22.880 --> 00:11:28.320
Louis’s troops made camp near the town, while the&nbsp;
prince himself took quarters in a small monastery&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:28.320 --> 00:11:33.680
that also served as his command center. He&nbsp;
spent several days surveying the terrain,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:33.680 --> 00:11:38.880
inspecting the defenses, and planning his assault.&nbsp;
According to the History of the Dukes of Normandy&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:38.880 --> 00:11:43.280
and the Kings of England, which contains a&nbsp;
detailed eyewitness account of the siege,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:43.280 --> 00:11:48.960
Louis then divided his forces into two groups. One&nbsp;
remained in the camp by the town, while the other&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:48.960 --> 00:11:54.720
moved to a hill near the castle’s northern gate,&nbsp;
where it began assembling the siege artillery.&nbsp;

00:11:54.720 --> 00:11:59.200
The French prince had high hopes for&nbsp;
his catapults, but faced with the mighty&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:59.200 --> 00:12:05.040
fortress before him, he also prepared a Plan&nbsp;
B. Troops not engaged in operating the siege&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:05.040 --> 00:12:09.280
engines encircled the castle, cutting it&nbsp;
off from the outside world and blocking&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:09.280 --> 00:12:14.480
any incoming supplies. At sea, Louis’s fleet&nbsp;
completed the stranglehold by patrolling the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:14.480 --> 00:12:20.640
coast. If the walls could not be breached,&nbsp;
he would starve the garrison into submission.&nbsp;

00:12:20.640 --> 00:12:26.720
With Dover Castle surrounded, the French focused&nbsp;
on the gatehouse. Alongside the catapults,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:26.720 --> 00:12:31.040
they constructed a siege tower to bring their&nbsp;
archers level with the defenders on the gates&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:31.040 --> 00:12:36.960
and ramparts. Workers also built a covered gallery&nbsp;
across the moat to the barbican—protection that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:36.960 --> 00:12:42.480
would later allow miners to dig beneath the wooden&nbsp;
wall. The siege of Rochester Castle the previous&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:42.480 --> 00:12:48.560
year had proven how effective this tactic could&nbsp;
be, even against massive stone structures. There,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:48.560 --> 00:12:53.120
King John had undermined the keep and then&nbsp;
packed the tunnel with 40 fat pigs before&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:53.120 --> 00:12:58.560
setting them ablaze to collapse the building.
The defenders of Dover Castle did not wait&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:58.560 --> 00:13:04.640
passively for the same thing to happen to them.&nbsp;
Hubert de Burgh led an active, almost aggressive&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:04.640 --> 00:13:10.080
defense. Time and again, his men-at-arms charged&nbsp;
out of the gates on horseback, cutting down a few&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:10.080 --> 00:13:16.240
besiegers before retreating at lightning speed.&nbsp;
From the walls, the defenders’ marksmen—especially&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:16.240 --> 00:13:21.840
the crossbowmen—kept the enemy at bay, picking&nbsp;
off workers in front of the barbican and cutting&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:21.840 --> 00:13:27.360
down anyone who ventured too close without cover.
The first three weeks of the siege passed with&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:27.360 --> 00:13:33.360
little progress for Louis. His army soon began&nbsp;
to dwindle. William, Count of Holland —who had&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:33.360 --> 00:13:39.360
supported the invasion—departed to join a crusade.&nbsp;
Many knights felt their duty fulfilled after so&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:39.360 --> 00:13:46.240
long abroad, and—unsurprisingly—the mercenaries&nbsp;
began grumbling over their wages. Despite his&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:46.240 --> 00:13:52.160
weakening grip on the situation, Louis vowed,&nbsp;
according to the chronicler Roger of Wendover,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:52.160 --> 00:14:00.240
“he would not leave the place till the castle&nbsp;
was taken and all the garrison were hung.”&nbsp;

00:14:00.240 --> 00:14:06.080
In mid-August, Louis finally achieved his first&nbsp;
breakthrough. The miners’ work paid off, bringing&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:06.080 --> 00:14:12.000
down part of the barbican. His men-at-arms surged&nbsp;
forward, and in a bloody clash they fought their&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:12.000 --> 00:14:18.000
way into the forecourt, driving back the defenders&nbsp;
and seizing the outer works. The first line of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:18.000 --> 00:14:25.120
defense had fallen—but the real challenge still&nbsp;
lay ahead. The impressive gateway towered before&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:25.120 --> 00:14:31.760
them, making their task look almost impossible.
Around the same time, King Alexander II of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:31.760 --> 00:14:37.360
Scotland traveled south to pay homage to Louis.&nbsp;
The French prince met the young monarch at&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:37.360 --> 00:14:42.880
Canterbury and seized the chance to strike a&nbsp;
psychological blow against the defenders. To&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:42.880 --> 00:14:48.720
undermine their morale, he invited Alexander to&nbsp;
Dover, where the homage was publicly repeated.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:48.720 --> 00:14:53.520
Louis already claimed the crown of England and&nbsp;
enjoyed the backing of many nobles, but this was&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:53.520 --> 00:14:59.120
the first time a reigning monarch of another&nbsp;
realm had formally acknowledged his status—a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:59.120 --> 00:15:04.560
gesture of enormous symbolic weight.
A few days after the ceremony, the Scottish&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:04.560 --> 00:15:09.360
king departed, and Louis turned his attention&nbsp;
back to the siege. Reinforcements arrived from&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:09.360 --> 00:15:14.720
France under Peter of Dreux and Thomas, Count of&nbsp;
Le Perche. They brought not only fresh men-at-arms&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:14.720 --> 00:15:21.600
but also grim news: Honorius III, the new pope,&nbsp;
had upheld his predecessor’s excommunication&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:21.600 --> 00:15:27.600
of Louis. It was a serious blow, but Louis&nbsp;
was now too deeply committed to turn back.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:27.600 --> 00:15:33.840
His only path was to conquer England, capture&nbsp;
John, and claim the crown. As King of England,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:33.840 --> 00:15:38.800
he hoped, he would wield the influence&nbsp;
needed to negotiate with the Pope.&nbsp;

00:15:38.800 --> 00:15:44.400
By early September, Louis’s goal seemed within&nbsp;
reach. The miners had nearly finished their&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:44.400 --> 00:15:49.920
tunnel beneath one of the two gate towers&nbsp;
and were preparing to bring it down. As the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:49.920 --> 00:15:54.720
workers made their final arrangements, the&nbsp;
men-at-arms readied themselves for battle.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:54.720 --> 00:16:04.240
Everything was in place for the final assault.
The ground shook as if from an earthquake,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:04.240 --> 00:16:09.760
and moments later, the tower collapsed with a&nbsp;
deafening crash. The attackers had been waiting&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:09.760 --> 00:16:15.600
for this moment—a chance to prove themselves and&nbsp;
unleash their frustration after the long siege. In&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:15.600 --> 00:16:21.920
a fury, they hurled themselves into the fight. The&nbsp;
defenders were just as resolute—for them, it was a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:21.920 --> 00:16:27.760
matter of life and death. Louis had sworn to hang&nbsp;
them all, and they had every reason to take that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:27.760 --> 00:16:34.000
threat seriously. With desperate strength, they&nbsp;
held the line, forcing Louis’s men-at-arms back&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:34.000 --> 00:16:39.920
step by step. As soon as the breach was clear,&nbsp;
workers rushed in with heavy beams to throw up&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:39.920 --> 00:16:45.760
a barricade. Despite the continued onslaught,&nbsp;
they managed to seal the gap. Realizing there&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:45.760 --> 00:16:55.120
was no way through, the French finally fell back.
After the failed assault, Louis was desperate. His&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:55.120 --> 00:17:00.960
men had spent over two months working toward&nbsp;
this moment, and it had all been in vain. The&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:00.960 --> 00:17:05.600
delay also weakened his position elsewhere&nbsp;
in England. Numerous barons loyal to the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:05.600 --> 00:17:10.640
king continued to resist, and the garrison of&nbsp;
Kent was being harassed by a group of archers&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:10.640 --> 00:17:15.200
under the squire William of Cassingham, who&nbsp;
employed tactics reminiscent of the legendary&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:15.200 --> 00:17:20.000
Robin Hood and his merry men. Engaging in a&nbsp;
form of guerrilla warfare, they repeatedly&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:20.000 --> 00:17:22.361
inflicted painful losses on the French.
Because William of Cassingham and his men&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:22.361 --> 00:17:25.120
continued to harass the French troops, Louis&nbsp;
needed to end the stalemate at Dover Castle&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:25.120 --> 00:17:30.800
quickly, or the invasion risked grinding to&nbsp;
a halt. With a new assault requiring weeks&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:30.800 --> 00:17:36.000
to prepare—and starving out the garrison likely&nbsp;
taking even longer—he had no choice but to sign&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:36.000 --> 00:17:45.200
a truce with Hubert de Burgh on October 14 and&nbsp;
lift the siege—for the time being, at least.&nbsp;

00:17:45.200 --> 00:17:52.400
Shortly afterward, on October 18, 1216,&nbsp;
King John “Lackland” died. Ten days later,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:52.400 --> 00:17:58.000
his nine-year-old son, Henry III, was crowned&nbsp;
in Gloucester Cathedral, with the experienced&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:58.000 --> 00:18:03.920
knight William Marshal appointed as regent. The&nbsp;
death of the old king changed everything. The&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:03.920 --> 00:18:09.040
tyrant the barons had opposed was gone. When&nbsp;
Marshal confirmed a slightly revised version&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:09.040 --> 00:18:14.640
of the Magna Carta on November 12, most of&nbsp;
the barons’ grievances were resolved, and an&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:14.640 --> 00:18:20.080
increasing number of them rallied to the young&nbsp;
Henry, recognizing him as their rightful king.&nbsp;

00:18:20.080 --> 00:18:25.360
In Dover, Louis tried to persuade the garrison&nbsp;
that it would be better to recognize him than to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:25.360 --> 00:18:30.560
place themselves under the rule of a child.&nbsp;
However, Hubert de Burgh had no intention&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:30.560 --> 00:18:36.160
of siding with the French prince. Declaring his&nbsp;
allegiance to Henry, he forced Louis to withdraw&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:36.160 --> 00:18:42.080
to London. Over the course of the winter, more&nbsp;
and more barons switched sides, and Louis found&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:42.080 --> 00:18:47.760
himself increasingly on the defensive until&nbsp;
early 1217, when he was forced to return to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:47.760 --> 00:18:54.080
France to fetch reinforcements. His march through&nbsp;
Kent and Sussex to the south coast nearly ended&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:54.080 --> 00:19:00.000
in disaster: the French army was starving because&nbsp;
resistance fighters systematically destroyed all&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:00.000 --> 00:19:06.720
food along their path and suffered heavy losses&nbsp;
in an ambush near Lewes. The remnants barely&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:06.720 --> 00:19:12.880
managed to reach a French fleet at Winchelsea.
In Louis’s absence, the Dover garrison treated the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:12.880 --> 00:19:19.040
truce lightly. The men burned the infrastructure&nbsp;
he had left behind, disrupted his communications&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:19.040 --> 00:19:24.560
with allies in England and France, and&nbsp;
repeatedly prevented French ships from landing.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:24.560 --> 00:19:28.960
They also raided the surrounding area to&nbsp;
replenish their stores and snatch vital&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:28.960 --> 00:19:35.520
resources from under Louis’s nose. When he&nbsp;
returned to England in April 1217, he resolved&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:35.520 --> 00:19:47.162
to put an end to their defiance. Dover Castle&nbsp;
had been a thorn in his side for far too long.&nbsp;

00:19:47.162 --> 00:19:50.080
Chapter 4: The Thorn in Louis’s Side
Louis reached Dover on May 12, prepared to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:50.080 --> 00:19:56.480
stay for weeks or even months. The French prince&nbsp;
was determined to take the fortress at any cost.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:56.480 --> 00:20:01.280
Knowing that Hubert de Burgh would ignore any&nbsp;
offer of surrender, he abandoned negotiations&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:01.280 --> 00:20:06.160
and immediately positioned his artillery.
In the meantime, he had reinforced his siege&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:06.160 --> 00:20:12.080
arsenal with a powerful new weapon: a massive&nbsp;
counterweight trebuchet sent by his father from&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:12.080 --> 00:20:17.920
France. According to historian John Goodall, this&nbsp;
was possibly the first trebuchet of its kind used&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:17.920 --> 00:20:24.400
in England. Its chief advantage over mangonels or&nbsp;
traction trebuchets was the ability to hurl much&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:24.400 --> 00:20:29.920
larger projectiles over greater distances. Still,&nbsp;
it was clear that even this formidable engine&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:29.920 --> 00:20:35.840
alone would not be enough to take Dover Castle.
With this second attack on Dover, Louis was&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:35.840 --> 00:20:41.600
taking a significant risk. He had to split his&nbsp;
forces: a large portion of his army remained&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:41.600 --> 00:20:45.760
with Thomas, Count of Le Perche, in front&nbsp;
of Lincoln. Although the town had fallen,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:45.760 --> 00:20:51.440
the castle stubbornly resisted. The siege of Dover&nbsp;
Castle consumed nearly all of the remaining troops&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:51.440 --> 00:20:57.200
Louis had in England. Consequently, he was heavily&nbsp;
dependent on reinforcements from France to sustain&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:57.200 --> 00:21:03.760
both sieges and avoid being exposed in the field.
At home, Blanche of Castile worked tirelessly&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:03.760 --> 00:21:08.720
to gather troops and funds to support her&nbsp;
husband. For the moment, she was only able&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:08.720 --> 00:21:14.880
to send 40 ships. Unfortunately, a violent storm&nbsp;
struck right as the small fleet reached England,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:14.880 --> 00:21:20.160
forcing it back out to sea. Two days&nbsp;
later, the weather had calmed, but the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:20.160 --> 00:21:25.200
delay had given young Henry’s supporters time&nbsp;
to prepare. When the French tried to land again,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:25.200 --> 00:21:30.800
the knight Philip d’Aubigny was waiting with 80&nbsp;
English ships. Some French captains turned back&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:30.800 --> 00:21:36.400
immediately at the sight of the superior force;&nbsp;
others attempted to break through. In the end,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:36.400 --> 00:21:42.080
only 19 ships made it to the shore—less than&nbsp;
half of the reinforcements Louis had hoped for.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:42.080 --> 00:21:48.160
Even worse, D’Aubigny now patrolled the coast,&nbsp;
creating a serious blockade. Until Blanche could&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:48.160 --> 00:21:55.280
send a fleet large enough to break it, Louis had&nbsp;
no choice but to make do with the forces he had.&nbsp;

00:21:55.280 --> 00:22:00.480
Meanwhile, William Marshal rallied the troops&nbsp;
loyal to Henry at Newark and marched on Lincoln&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:22:00.480 --> 00:22:06.800
to liberate the city. He reached his destination&nbsp;
on May 20 and dealt a crushing defeat to Le&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:22:06.800 --> 00:22:14.240
Perche. When news of this reached Dover five days&nbsp;
later, Louis was devastated. With the loss of his&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:22:14.240 --> 00:22:21.760
men at Lincoln, roughly half of his troops were&nbsp;
gone, and with them the military advantage. Also,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:22:21.760 --> 00:22:28.720
Dover still held out, and Marshal was preparing&nbsp;
to advance on London. Louis’s position seemed&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:22:28.720 --> 00:22:34.000
hopeless. He dismantled his siege engines&nbsp;
and set off toward London to begin peace&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:22:34.000 --> 00:22:39.360
negotiations with Henry’s supporters.&nbsp;
Just as an agreement was within reach,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:22:39.360 --> 00:22:45.280
however, unexpected news arrived from France:&nbsp;
Blanche and her allies had managed to assemble&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:22:45.280 --> 00:22:52.640
a sizeable fleet after all. Buoyed by this&nbsp;
development, Louis decided to continue the fight.&nbsp;

00:22:52.640 --> 00:22:57.680
The flame of hope flickered only briefly. When&nbsp;
the French reinforcements crossed the Strait of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:22:57.680 --> 00:23:03.920
Dover on August 24, Hubert de Burgh was already&nbsp;
lying in wait with an English fleet in the port&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:23:03.920 --> 00:23:09.920
of Sandwich. He launched a surprise attack on the&nbsp;
passing French ships, delivering a crushing defeat&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:23:09.920 --> 00:23:15.840
and extinguishing the last spark of hope.
After the defeat in the Battle of Sandwich,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:23:15.840 --> 00:23:22.960
Louis finally gave up. On September 11, 1217,&nbsp;
he signed the Treaty of Lambeth, renounced all&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:23:22.960 --> 00:23:28.640
claims to the English throne, and returned to&nbsp;
France. Dover had not only been a thorn in his&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:23:28.640 --> 00:23:34.480
side but also a decisive stronghold for the&nbsp;
English resistance. The invasion had failed,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:23:34.480 --> 00:23:44.960
and England had taken a decisive step toward&nbsp;
its constitutional future with the Magna Carta.

